🐳 Docker Hands-On: Updating a Containerized Application

As part of my DevOps learning journey, we are on a journey of building a Docker image, deploying a container, exposing application ports, and testing application connectivity.
The objective was to containerize an application and make it accessible through the host machine.

💡 Key DevOps Learnings
Through this practical exercise, I strengthened my understanding of:
✅ Docker image creation and versioning
✅ Running containers in detached mode
✅ Port mapping and container networking
✅ Updating application source files
✅ Building a new image version (v1 → v2)
✅ Removing and recreating containers
✅ Deploying updated application versions
✅ Validating deployments using curl
🚀 Real-World DevOps Connection


This exercise demonstrates a fundamental application deployment workflow:

Source Code Update → Docker Image Build → Container Replacement → Application Verification

In production environments, similar concepts are implemented through CI/CD pipelines and container orchestration platforms such as Kubernetes.

🔹 Step 1: Build the Docker Image
Created a Docker image named app/v1 using the Dockerfile.
docker build -t app:v1 .

 
What I learned:
•	How to build Docker images.
•	How to assign image names and tags.
•	How Docker uses a Dockerfile to package an application.
🔹 Step 2: Run the Container
Started a container named app using the newly created image.
docker run -d -p 3000:3000 --name app app:v1
 

Understanding the flags:
•	-d – Runs the container in detached mode (background).
•	-p 3000:3000 – Maps host port 3000 to container port 3000.
•	--name app – Assigns a custom name to the container.
•	App:v1 – Specifies the Docker image.

 
🔹 Step 3: Test Application Connectivity
Verified that the application was accessible using:
curl localhost:3000
 
This confirmed that the application was responding through the exposed port.

 

🔹 Part 2: Update and Redeploy the Application (v2)
Next, I simulated a real-world application update by modifying the application content and deploying a new Docker image.
Step 1: Update Application Content
Appended a new HTML heading to /root/index.html.
echo '<h2>Some updates for app/v2</h2>' >> /root/index.html
 
Step 2: Build the Updated Docker Image
Created a new image version without modifying the existing v1 image.
docker build -t app/v2 -f . 

 
Step 3: Remove the Existing Container
Removed the old v1 container before deploying the updated version.
docker rm -f <container_id/container_name>
 
Step 4: Deploy the Updated Application
Started a new container using the app/v2 image.
docker run -d -p 3000:3000 --name app app/v2
 
Step 5: Verify the Updated Application
curl localhost:3000
Verified the updated application response, including the new HTML heading:
<h2>Some updates for app/v2</h2>
 
 

